1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,500
Every click becomes a dashboard number
6 steps in between

2
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:25,000
Kafka, streaming, watermarks
Idempotency, medallion, pruning

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Clickstream: one row per user action
event_id, user_id, event_type

4
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:55,000
event_time: when it happened
ingest_time: when we received it

5
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:16,500
Our events table: 9 rows, 8 events
Event 3 arrives twice (a retry)

6
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:38,000
Event 6 arrives 17 min 40 s late

7
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Kafka doesn't order your topic.
It orders each partition.

8
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:06,500
Topic: a named stream of events
Split into partitions across brokers

9
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:22,000
One partition: read in the order written
Across partitions: no order promised

10
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Same key, same partition
Key = user_id

11
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:56,000
User 101: view, click, purchase in order

12
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Consumer group: consumers sharing a topic
Each partition: one consumer in the group

13
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Position: one offset per partition

14
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:35,500
Q: How do you keep a user's events in order?
A: key by user_id

15
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Order is per partition, not per topic

16
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Takeaway: order lives in the partition
Pick the key that matches your question

17
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Batch or streaming?
First decide which clock you trust.

18
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:16,500
Batch: process what has landed, then stop
Streaming: keep processing as events arrive

19
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:29,000
Same events, different trigger

20
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:49,500
Event time: the time inside the data
Ingest time: when it reached us

21
00:04:49,500 --> 00:05:10,000
Event 6: 17 min 40 s between them

22
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:21,500
Spark tracks how far each stream has read
Offsets + checkpoints: a replayable source

23
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:33,000
Restart: pick up from the checkpoint

24
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:47,000
Ask first: how fresh must the numbers be?
Let the requirement pick the mode

25
00:05:47,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Either way: count by event_time

26
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:12,000
Takeaway: ask how fresh it needs to be
Event time picks the bucket

27
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:22,000
Late data isn't automatically dropped.
The watermark decides.

28
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:32,500
When is the 09:00 window finished?
A straggler could always show up

29
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:43,000
Threshold: how late can data be?

30
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:58,000
watermark = max event time seen - threshold
Less late than threshold: guaranteed

31
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:13,000
Later than that: best effort, may drop

32
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:27,000
Illustration in SQL, not Spark itself
Event 6 arrives: max seen is 09:06:40

33
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:41,000
Only 2 min 40 s behind in event time
10 min threshold: guaranteed

34
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:55,000
2 min threshold: best effort

35
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:08,000
Lateness is measured in event time
Not wall-clock delay

36
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:21,000
Watermark moves only with newer events

37
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Takeaway: watermark = max event time - threshold
Inside: guaranteed. Outside: best effort.

38
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:39,000
Assume your pipeline runs twice.
Make twice look like once.

39
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:51,000
Idempotent: run twice = run once
Retries and replays are normal

40
00:08:51,000 --> 00:09:03,000
Event 3 already arrived twice

41
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:18,500
Kafka idempotent producer
Producer ID + sequence number

42
00:09:18,500 --> 00:09:34,000
Broker spots the resend and dedupes it

43
00:09:34,000 --> 00:09:57,000
DuckDB: PRIMARY KEY (event_id)
INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING

44
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Load 9 rows, then retry the load
Result: 8 rows, one per event

45
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Spark exactly-once: two ingredients
1. Replayable source (offsets, checkpoints)

46
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:40,000
2. Idempotent sink
Replay safely, write safely

47
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Inside the stream: dropDuplicates
With watermark: key on event_id + event_time

48
00:10:52,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Takeaway: assume every step runs twice
Key on event_id. Make the sink idempotent.

49
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Your dashboard says 4 clicks.
There were 3.

50
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:20,000
Bronze: raw, unvalidated, as it arrived
Silver: cleaned, validated, deduplicated

51
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:30,000
Gold: aggregated for dashboards and BI

52
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:48,000
Count clicks straight from bronze
click: 4 (event 3 counted twice)

53
00:11:48,000 --> 00:12:04,500
ROW_NUMBER() per event_id
Keep rn = 1: the first arrival

54
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:21,000
9 rows in, 8 rows out

55
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:33,500
GROUP BY event_date, event_type
click 3, page_view 3, purchase 2

56
00:12:33,500 --> 00:12:46,000
Distinct users with COUNT(DISTINCT)

57
00:12:46,000 --> 00:13:06,000
Why keep bronze?
Rules change: rebuild silver from raw

58
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:12,000
Takeaway: bronze keeps, silver fixes,
gold answers.

59
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:22,000
Pruning needs a filter on the partition column.
Miss it, and no partition is skipped.

60
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:32,000
Partition silver by event_date
Each day stored in its own partition

61
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:42,000
Lake: event_date=2026-10-19/ folders

62
00:13:42,000 --> 00:14:02,500
WHERE event_date = DATE '2026-10-19'
Filter on partition column: skip the rest

63
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:23,000
BigQuery: fewer bytes processed
DuckDB: filter pushed into Hive partitions

64
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:34,500
Filter only on user_id? No partition skipped
The filter must hit the partition column

65
00:14:34,500 --> 00:14:46,000
Partition by what you filter by

66
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:52,000
Takeaway: partition by what you filter by

67
00:14:52,000 --> 00:15:05,000
Kafka: key by user_id, order per partition
Clock: count by event_time

68
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:18,000
Watermark: max event time - threshold
Idempotent: replayable source + keyed sink

69
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:31,000
Medallion: bronze, silver, gold
Pruning: filter on event_date

70
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:43,000
More visual explanations: queryunstuck.in
Data & AI Engineering, visualized.
